{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "hardware_incident_spike",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "hardware_equipment_class",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The class of hardware (system/printer/network) is a candidate cause whose distribution could explain why hardware incidents spiked; it is a plausible driver of the outcome surfaced from the data."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mechanism",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The failure mechanism (update_failure, connectivity_lost) is a direct candidate cause of the incident spike; e.g. a wave of update_failures could attribute the spike. This is the most direct treatment/cause variable."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_pattern",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Describes whether incidents repeat; this is more a characterization/consequence of the spike than a common cause of both a treatment and the outcome. Closer to a descriptive marker of the outcome than a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_technician",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Technician workload/skill can plausibly influence which failures get logged/resolved and correlate with incident counts, but its status as a genuine common cause of both a treatment and the outcome is weak; often it is downstream of the incident (a collider-ish assignment)."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_identity",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Certain callers/departments may use particular hardware and be more prone to report incidents, plausibly a common cause of both equipment class exposure and reporting of incidents; surfaced from data."
    },
    {
      "name": "urgency_impact_level",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Urgency/impact is a downstream consequence/severity rating assigned to an incident, on the effect side rather than a common cause; adjusting for it would be a mistake."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["failure_mechanism", "hardware_equipment_class"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "caller_identity", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4},
    {"name": "assigned_technician", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.3}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.3,
  "rationale": "The columns clearly supply candidate TREATMENTS (failure_mechanism, hardware_equipment_class) that could attribute the spike, and none of the confounders were named in the query. However, the set is weak on genuine CONFOUNDERS -- common causes of BOTH treatment and outcome. Notably absent is any temporal/exposure variable (e.g., rollout timing, patch/change events, volume/load) that would confound the anomaly-window attribution. The proposed confounders (caller_identity, assigned_technician) are only weakly plausible as common causes and lean toward downstream assignment/reporting, while urgency_impact_level and recurrence_pattern sit on the outcome/collider side. The operator set up a treatment but did not convincingly surface adjustment-worthy confounders, so causal attribution is only partially realised."
}

